+ TableOp is U32

` What a node planned `plan-table` does to a table or a view of one, as TypeScript answers it
` before any method: `t[i]` makes a row, a field of a row is read or written in its column,
` `t[i] = v` scatters a row into the columns, `t.f` makes a view of a field's column,
` `length` and `size` count the rows or the items, `t[a .. < b]` makes a span of the rows or
` the items a range spans, `f[i]` reads an item of a field's column and `f[i] = v` writes one,
` `f.data` is where the column's items start, `f + g` makes the tuple of the views of fields'
` columns that a span is cast from, and `t[a .. < b] = s` copies the rows or the items of a
` span or a view into those a range spans. The members of an arena's table are answered where
` they are called: `append`, `reserve`, `extend`, `truncate`, `clear`, `copy-from`, `finish`,
` `view`, `rewind`, `storage-checkpoint`, `validate-storage-restore` and `restore-storage`, and the
` getters `mark` and `capacity`. `t[ids]` selects rows by indices; `s[i]` and `s[a .. < b]` of
` an index span read an index and span some, and of a selection read a row and span some.
` `t.layout` is the text of how a table keeps its rows. The
` node's constant holds the operation in its low half and the place of the field in the row's
` layout in its high half.

| table-row = 0
| table-field = 1
| table-store = 2
| table-column = 3
| table-length = 4
| table-span = 5
| table-item = 6
| table-item-store = 7
| table-data = 8
| table-zip = 9
| table-range-store = 10
| table-append = 11
